      "target": {
        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(security): validate port range in suspicious-network scanner rule",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nThe `suspicious-network` scanner rule uses `Number.parseInt` on a regex capture group to extract a port number, then checks `STANDARD_PORTS.has(port)`. If `parseInt` returns `NaN` (e.g. from unexpected regex match content), `STANDARD_PORTS.has(NaN)` returns `false`, so the finding is reported with a `NaN` port — a false positive with a meaningless value. Negative ports and values exceeding 65535 are also not valid TCP/UDP ports but would bypass the standard-port allowlist.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nAdd `Number.isFinite(port) && port >= 0 && port <= 65535` before the `STANDARD_PORTS` lookup so that only valid, in-range port numbers can trigger the standard-port skip. Out-of-range or non-numeric values are now reported as findings with their original evidence, which is more useful than a NaN port number.\n\n## User Impact\n\nMinor — eliminates false-positive scanner findings with NaN port numbers. No change to legitimate findings.\n\n## Evidence\n\n```text\n$ npx vitest run src/skills/security/scanner.test.ts\n Test Files  1 passed (1)\n      Tests  20 passed (20)\n```\n\n## AI-assisted\n\nNo.",
